---
/*
 * Ways to play: the rules that turn a prompt deck into a card game. The
 * five games here also exist as printed instruction cards — condensed
 * versions live in scripts/print-cards.js (RULES_CARDS); keep the two in
 * step when a game changes.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { DECK_NAME, PHYSICAL_DECK_URL } from "../config";

const description =
  "Five ways to play Grasping Straws — solo rituals, a duet, and studio games for working artists.";
const physicalHref = PHYSICAL_DECK_URL || "/about/#physical";
---

<Base title={`Ways to play — ${DECK_NAME}`} description={description} bodyClass="about">
  <main>
    <a class="backlink" href="/">&larr; Back to the deck</a>

    <span class="glyph page-mark"></span>

    <h1>Ways to play</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      A card on its own is a nudge. A rule about when you draw, what you owe the card, and who else
      is holding one turns the deck into a game. Five games, tested against real deadlocks. The only
      rule they share: the deck doesn't negotiate.
    </p>

    <h2>Ten Minutes <span class="players">solo</span></h2>
    <p>
      The basic ritual. When the work stalls, draw one card and obey it — literally, not
      approximately — for ten minutes. No second draw, no interpreting your way back to what you
      were already doing. If the card seems to make no sense for your medium, that gap is the
      exercise: a drummer holding <em>&ldquo;Mute the loudest layer&rdquo;</em> has more to decide than
      a mixer does. After ten minutes you may keep what happened or throw it away, but you have to look
      at it first.
    </p>

    <h2>Three Straws <span class="players">solo</span></h2>
    <p>
      A spread, for when you don't know why you're stuck. Deal three cards face down. Turn them one
      at a time: the first names what is actually wrong &mdash; read it as a diagnosis, not an
      instruction. The second is how to work on it. The third is your stopping rule: the condition
      under which the piece is finished whether you agree or not. Write the third one down where you
      can see it. Most deadlocks are a finishing problem wearing a starting problem's clothes.
    </p>

    <h2>First &amp; Last <span class="players">solo, one session</span></h2>
    <p>
      Draw a card before you begin and let it set the session's terms. Work as long as you like.
      When you believe you're done, draw exactly one more and let it edit the ending &mdash; it may
      cost you the last hour or save the whole day. You agreed to this before you started; that's
      why it works. The first card opens the session, the last one closes it, and neither is your
      taste.
    </p>

    <h2>Blind Jury <span class="players">3 or more</span></h2>
    <p>
      One card, read aloud, for everyone. No one says how they intend to read it. Twenty minutes of
      work in whatever medium each person brought, then show the results side by side and only then
      say what you each thought the card meant. The game is the divergence: the same sentence sent
      five people five directions, and at least one of those directions is better than yours. Steal
      it. Rotate who draws.
    </p>

    <h2>Exquisite Relay <span class="players">2, over days or weeks</span></h2>
    <p>
      Two artists, one piece, passed back and forth. Each time the piece changes hands, the receiver
      draws a card in secret and works under it; the sender must not know which instruction shaped
      the pass. Keep the drawn cards in order, face down, like a growing spine. When the piece is
      finished, reveal the sequence and read it as the piece's secret history. Title the work with
      one of the cards.
    </p>

    <h2>House rules</h2>
    <p>
      These five are openings, not the whole book. The deck holds five suits &mdash; on the
      <a href="/">draw screen</a> you can deal from just one &mdash; and any of these games can be played
      inside a single suit when the deadlock has a known shape. If you invent a game worth keeping, it's
      yours: the deck is the instrument, not the score.
    </p>

    <p class="credit">
      <a href="/about/">About the deck</a> &middot;
      <a href={physicalHref}>Get the physical deck</a>
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
